# A string joined onto itself is a builder, and the seed is converted where it
# enters the group from outside, because a builder writes into the header it
# was given. The group's own recursive call is exempt: it already holds one.
# A call arriving from anywhere else re-seeds, and seeding copies the whole
# string, so passing the accumulator through one intermediate function costs a
# copy every iteration: bytes_malloc is 40 for these 40 appends, against one
# for the same appends written without the hop.
#
# Skipping the seed wherever the header still has room takes that to 25, and
# it is unsound. Spare capacity does not mean sole ownership — `text/join`
# over-allocates, so its result arrives with room to write while the parts it
# was built from are still live — and a builder that trusts it writes into a
# buffer somebody else holds. Two panels of a book chapter rewritten in one
# pass took 88 GB and a SIGKILL that way. What a builder needs before it can
# claim a header is a uniqueness it can prove, which is a real piece of work
# and not a comparison of two integers.
fn build_onto acc 0
  acc

fn build_onto acc n
  grown = "{acc}xx"
  handed grown n

fn handed grown n
  build_onto grown (n - 1)

pub play = print (build_onto "" 40)
